    "body": "<!-- claude-projects-attribution: {\"github_login\":\"siainbuletin\"} -->\n_Requested by **Sia**_\n\n## Summary\n- `sp-ai` is no longer in the default module list in `init.el`, so none of the AI packages (gptel, claude-code, eca, agent-shell, efrit, monet, vulpea-mcp, org-mcp) load or install at startup.\n- New command `M-x sp-ai-load` requires `sp-ai`, then calls `elpaca-wait` so Elpaca installs and activates what is missing before it returns. Calling it twice is harmless.\n- `docs/tangles/media/sp-ai.org` now says the MCP servers start with `sp-ai-load`, including for the headless daemon.\n\n## Why\nIn batch runs of the config, `sp-ai` was by far the slowest module (11–16 s of a 26 s init, 38 s in one run), mostly Elpaca work for its git-sourced packages.\n\n## Effect to be aware of\nThe `vulpea-mcp` and `org-mcp` servers used for Claude's MCP access are no longer up at startup. They start after `sp-ai-load` (for a daemon: `emacsclient -s mcp -e '(sp-ai-load)'`).\n\n## Not tested\n`init.el` was only checked for balanced parentheses (`check-parens` in batch). I have not run a GUI Emacs, so `sp-ai-load` has not been tried interactively.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
